## What you just described, in order

**1. Site-to-site internal transfer (the "almost the same as warehouse" case)**
Site X's engineer needs a tindo, scraper, drilling machine. Site Y has them idle. Site Y's storekeeper hands them over with a delivery note — reference number, items, who authorized it, who delivered, who received. But the actual verification is weak: if the note says "9 units" and only 5 or 13 actually arrive, nobody's counting against the paper in real time. The document exists for accountability _after the fact_, not as a live check. Trust, not verification, is what's actually running that handoff.

**2. Site → Main Warehouse, when nothing's on any site**
This starts informally — WhatsApp, not a system. Main warehouse storekeeper checks their own stock, doesn't have it either, and _verbally_ escalates to procurement. You said it yourself: "in reality they should make an order, this is my thinking, not sure" — meaning even you know the real-world process is looser than what "should" happen on paper. This is important — it tells me the digital system can't assume every step is formally logged, because it isn't today.

**3. Procurement is a judgment layer, not a rubber stamp**
Bharti doesn't just buy what's asked. She asks _why_ ("what's this paint for?"), checks with Dipesh (a second internal check — availability/priority), and ultimately the answer comes from the Director on money grounds — "pause until we're paid" or "only buy 60 of the 100." That's a **real approval chain with real partial-approval outcomes**, driven by cash position, not by budget lines in a spreadsheet. This is your Finance/cash-position gate (item #13 on the earlier list) — confirmed as a real, lived thing, not a hypothetical.

**4. The LPO and the supplier relationship**
Once approved, an LPO goes to Crown. Crown doesn't know or care about "Lake Vitoria Project" — they only know they're delivering to _the company_. Their delivery note carries their own reference (items, qty, rates, LPO number) — not yours. That's a crucial detail: **the supplier's paperwork and your internal project tracking are two separate document trails that only connect through the LPO reference number.**

**5. Delivery can skip the warehouse entirely**
Bharti can tell Crown "just take it straight to site" — bypassing the office. The delivery note _should_ still route back to the office (via the company driver) so it gets reconciled, but the physical goods and the paperwork can literally travel different paths and arrive at different times. That's a real desync risk baked into how it works today.

**6. Receiving at Main Warehouse is trust-based, not systemized**
"Checked count and in" — no cross-referencing against the original LPO quantity at the point of receiving. Whatever arrives is treated as correct. Then the storekeeper writes _a new_ delivery note to send it onward to site — so now there are potentially three separate documents in play for one paint order: Crown's delivery note, the warehouse's internal receipt, and the warehouse-to-site delivery note.

**7. Downstream — billing and valuation**
Later, procurement batches up all of Crown's delivery notes and hands them to Jilna (accountant), who turns them into an invoice. That invoice then goes to Zena (QS), who uses it to build the valuation. You flagged this as "for later" — noted, not building it now, but it confirms the eventual endpoint: **everything that happened on the ground eventually has to reconcile into the QS's valuation and the accountant's invoice.**

